(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-cms-exam-6-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 6 Master Exam — Form B","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with confirmed chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a symptom assessment test score of 18 and a modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade of 2. She has had no exacerbations in the past year. Under the current three-group scheme, which group is she in?","choices":["Group B","Group E","Group A","Group D"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A symptom score of 18 (10 or more) and a dyspnea grade of 2 (2 or more) mean more symptoms, and no exacerbations means low risk. More symptoms with low risk is group B, which starts on a long-acting beta agonist plus a long-acting antimuscarinic. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 37, 40, 41"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has had a cough for 10 days that began with a cold. Her sputum is green, and she asks for an antibiotic. Her temperature, heart rate, respiratory rate and oxygen saturation are normal, and her lungs have scattered rhonchi that clear with coughing and no focal findings. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Chest radiograph and sputum culture","Supportive care, no antibiotic","Levofloxacin","Amoxicillin-clavulanate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Normal vital signs, no focal lung findings and rhonchi that clear with a cough describe uncomplicated acute bronchitis, usually a self-limited viral illness. Green or yellow sputum does not separate viral from bacterial infection, so no antibiotic is given; explain the expected course and agree on symptom goals. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 16, 18, 19, 22"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man with extrinsic asthma flares whenever he cleans his apartment, and allergy skin testing shows sensitivity to house dust mites. His controller and rescue inhalers are prescribed. Which non-drug measure should be added?","choices":["Move from the city to a rural area","Limit his physical activity","Reduce dust mite exposure at home","Start allergen immunotherapy at home"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Non-pharmacological asthma treatment is trigger avoidance and environmental control. Dust mites are the most common inhaled allergen trigger, and his skin test and cleaning-related flares identify it, so reducing his exposure at home is the added measure. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 38, 67"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with ventilator-associated pneumonia was started on cefepime plus vancomycin because methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is common in her unit. On day 3 her respiratory culture grows Pseudomonas susceptible to cefepime, and no Staphylococcus aureus grows. She is improving. What is the most appropriate change?","choices":["Add a second antipseudomonal class","Switch to moxifloxacin","Continue both drugs for 14 days","Stop vancomycin, continue cefepime"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her culture shows cefepime-susceptible Pseudomonas and no Staphylococcus aureus, and she is improving, so therapy is de-escalated to the culture and susceptibility results: the vancomycin added for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is stopped and cefepime continues, usually for 7 days in total. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 40, 42, 46"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with chronic lung disease is drowsy and confused, with asterixis (a flapping tremor of the outstretched hands). Arterial blood gas: pH 7.22 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 75 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 30 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder best explains his findings?","choices":["Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis","Metabolic acidosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.22 is acidemia, and the carbon dioxide of 75 is high and matches it, so it is respiratory acidosis. Bicarbonate of 30 is the renal response. Somnolence, confusion and asterixis are its listed clinical findings, and chronic lung disease fits. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man with newly confirmed chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a symptom assessment test score of 6, a modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade of 1, and no exacerbations in the past year. Which initial medication is most appropriate?","choices":["A single bronchodilator","Dual long-acting bronchodilators","An inhaled corticosteroid alone","Daily oral corticosteroids"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A score of 6 and a dyspnea grade of 1 mean few symptoms, and no exacerbations means low risk, so he is group A, which starts with 1 bronchodilator, short- or long-acting. He still needs smoking cessation if he smokes, and physical activity. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 37, 40, 49"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is taught to breathe out slowly through pursed lips when she is short of breath. She asks why it helps. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["It increases inspiratory flow","It slows her heart rate","It reduces mucus production","It keeps her airways open"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pursed-lip breathing increases intra-airway pressure, which keeps the airways open during expiration and allows more gas exchange. It counters the collapse of airways that loss of elastic recoil causes in emphysema. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man has hypertension treated with a thiazide diuretic, stable chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, and osteoarthritis for which he takes aspirin. He feels weak. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.48 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 46 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 33 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); potassium 3.1 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L); urine chloride is low. Which part of his history best accounts for this blood gas?","choices":["His thiazide diuretic","Primary aldosteronism","His aspirin","His chronic lung disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. High pH with a high bicarbonate is metabolic alkalosis, and the low urine chloride marks the saline-responsive type. Diuretic therapy is a saline-responsive cause, and the low potassium and weakness fit; the carbon dioxide of 46 is the expected compensation. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 33, 39, 41, 42, 43, 59"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman who works in a setting with ongoing tuberculosis transmission has a positive interferon-gamma release assay on targeted testing. She has no symptoms, her examination is normal and her chest radiograph is normal. Which best describes her tuberculosis status?","choices":["Latent tuberculosis infection","Progressive primary tuberculosis","Reactivation (secondary) tuberculosis","Pulmonary tuberculosis disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A positive interferon-gamma release assay shows infection, and her clinical evaluation found no evidence of disease: no symptoms, a normal examination and a normal radiograph, which is usual in latent infection. Latent infection is asymptomatic and not infectious. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 10, 19, 29"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man is recovering in the hospital from an exacerbation. He has been clinically stable for 18 hours, walks across the room, eats and sleeps without waking from breathlessness, and his home oxygen is arranged, but he still needs albuterol every 2 hours. Which finding delays his discharge?","choices":["Stable for 18 hours","Albuterol needed every 2 hours","Walking across the room","Home oxygen arranged"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A discharge criterion is needing inhaled short-acting beta-2 agonist no more often than every 4 hours. Every 2 hours means his airflow is not yet controlled enough for home, even though his other criteria are met. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man has had mild asthma since childhood, controlled with occasional albuterol. He asks whether his asthma is likely to become severe as he ages. Which reply is most accurate?","choices":["Mild asthma rarely becomes severe","Severity rises steadily with each decade","Severe asthma usually begins mild","Most mild asthma turns severe within a decade"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Asthma severity rarely varies for a given patient: mild asthma rarely becomes severe, and severe asthma is usually severe at onset. His long history of mild disease predicts it will most likely stay mild. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man's family turned his home nasal cannula up to 9 L/min because he felt short of breath. About what fraction of inspired oxygen does he receive at that setting?","choices":["44%","56%","60%","100%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A nasal cannula can be turned to any setting, but the fraction stops at 44% (reached at 6 L/min). At 9 L/min he gets no more than that, so the extra 3 liters add oxygen to his nose but not to his fraction. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slides 9, 11, 14, 15"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 45% predicted, a 6-minute walk distance of 200 meters, a modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade of 3, and a body mass index of 20. What is his BODE (body mass index, obstruction, dyspnea, exercise) index score?","choices":["5","6","7","8"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. 1-second volume 45% (36 to 49) = 2 + walk 200 meters (150 to 249) = 2 + dyspnea grade 3 = 2 + body mass index 20 (21 or less) = 1, for a total of 7. A score of 7 to 10 predicts about 18% 4-year survival. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with heart disease has a severe metabolic alkalosis from vomiting. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.56 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 49 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 43 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). How does her alkalemia affect oxygen delivery to her tissues?","choices":["It increases delivery","It shifts the curve to the right","It does not change delivery","It decreases delivery"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Alkalosis increases hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen and shifts the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the left, so hemoglobin holds oxygen and delivery to the tissues decreases. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"In an eligible adult, what usually follows a dose of the 15-valent pneumococcal conjugate vaccine?","choices":["A 23-valent polysaccharide dose","A second 15-valent conjugate dose","Nothing; the series is complete","A yearly pneumococcal booster"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The 15-valent conjugate vaccine usually requires the 23-valent polysaccharide vaccine afterward, usually 1 year or more later. A 20- or 21-valent conjugate vaccine, by contrast, completes the series for an adult who has never had a conjugate vaccine. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 8, 9, 124"},{"q":"For suspected Pneumocystis pneumonia, which respiratory specimen is obtained first when it is feasible?","choices":["Spontaneously expectorated sputum","Lung biopsy tissue","Induced sputum","Bronchoalveolar lavage fluid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Induced sputum is obtained for microscopy and/or polymerase chain reaction when feasible; if it is negative or unavailable and suspicion remains high, bronchoalveolar lavage follows. A negative induced specimen does not exclude Pneumocystis pneumonia. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 65, 66, 67, 69"},{"q":"Which term names a pathologic condition with excessively alkaline body fluids or tissues?","choices":["Hypocapnia","Alkalemia","Acidosis","Alkalosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Alkalosis is the pathologic condition of excessively alkaline body fluids or tissues. The resulting blood finding, a pH above 7.45, is called alkalemia, so the condition and the finding have different names. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Smoking-related emphysema typically follows which pattern?","choices":["Bronchial wall thickening","Panlobular, lower lobes","Diffuse fibrosis at the bases","Centrilobular, upper lobes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Emphysema from smoking is centrilobular and predominantly in the upper lobes. The panlobular, lower-lobe pattern belongs to alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, which is why basilar emphysema prompts testing for it. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"How is psittacosis primarily acquired?","choices":["Circulating water droplets","Soil with bird droppings","Person-to-person droplets","Inhaled from sick birds"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Psittacosis, caused by the obligate intracellular bacterium Chlamydia psittaci, is acquired mainly through the respiratory system when handling sick birds, or by mouth-to-beak resuscitation. Human-to-human transmission is rare. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 81, 80"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman develops ventilator-associated pneumonia on day 5 of mechanical ventilation. Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is common in her unit. Which empiric regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin","Cefepime plus vancomycin","Moxifloxacin plus linezolid","Cefepime as a single drug"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Local methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus prevalence adds vancomycin or linezolid to an antipseudomonal beta-lactam such as cefepime. Cultures are obtained, and therapy is reassessed daily and de-escalated. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 46, 42, 48"},{"q":"Which group uses a tuberculin skin test induration cut-off of 5 mm or more?","choices":["Children under 5 years","People with no risk factors","Recent close contacts","Residents of congregate settings"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The 5 mm cut-off applies to human immunodeficiency virus infection, recent close contacts, transplant recipients, major immunosuppression, and imaging suggesting prior tuberculosis. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with Guillain-Barré syndrome has worsening weakness and shallow breathing. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.26 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 58 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 26 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What is the priority treatment for his acid-base disorder?","choices":["Careful oxygen titration alone","Restore adequate ventilation","Give sodium bicarbonate to raise the pH","Wait for renal compensation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A high carbon dioxide with a low pH and a barely raised bicarbonate is acute respiratory acidosis, and Guillain-Barré syndrome is a listed neuromuscular cause. Acute respiratory acidosis is treated by restoring ventilation while the cause is treated. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"On an arterial blood gas, which change shows a good response to oxygen therapy?","choices":["Lower arterial oxygen with a lower pH","Unchanged oxygen with a higher pH","Higher arterial oxygen with a rise in carbon dioxide","Higher arterial oxygen with no rise in carbon dioxide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A good response on an arterial blood gas is an increase in the arterial oxygen pressure without an increase in the carbon dioxide pressure, together with correction of the pH. Oxygen should help without causing retention. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman hospitalized with coronavirus disease 2019 and hypoxemia has an elevated D-dimer. She has no signs of venous thrombosis and no bleeding risk. Which anticoagulation plan is most appropriate?","choices":["Therapeutic-dose anticoagulation","Prophylactic-dose anticoagulation","Hold anticoagulants until imaging","No anticoagulation for now"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hospitalized patients receive venous thromboembolism prophylaxis unless contraindicated. Therapeutic dosing requires a thrombosis and bleeding assessment, and an elevated D-dimer alone neither diagnoses thrombosis nor sets the dose. She has no thrombosis, so the prophylactic dose fits. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 117, 121"},{"q":"A 20-year-old man has daily asthma symptoms and uses albuterol daily, but he wakes at night only once a month and has a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 85% predicted. He has some limitation playing pickup basketball. Which severity classification fits?","choices":["Mild persistent","Moderate persistent","Intermittent","Severe persistent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Daily symptoms, daily short-acting beta-2 agonist use and some limitation of activity are moderate persistent features. Even though his night waking and 85% function are milder, severity is assigned to the most severe category in which any feature occurs. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has spirometry for suspected asthma. Before a bronchodilator her forced expiratory volume in 1 second is 61% predicted with a ratio of 55%; after albuterol it is 94% predicted, a 54% improvement. Which interpretation fits?","choices":["Reversible airflow obstruction","Fixed airflow obstruction","Poor patient effort","Normal spirometry"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A reduced 1-second volume and a reduced ratio before the bronchodilator show airflow limitation, and a 54% improvement after albuterol, far above the 12% threshold, shows reversibility. A positive bronchodilator response strongly supports asthma. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 53, 54, 57"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman has a metabolic alkalosis with normal blood pressure; she denies vomiting, diuretics and antacids. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.48 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 46 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 32 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which test separates the saline-responsive from the saline-unresponsive type?","choices":["Winter formula","Serum albumin","Urine chloride","Anion gap"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Urine chloride differentiates the two types: it is low (below 25 mEq/L) in saline-responsive alkalosis and high (above 40 mEq/L) in saline-unresponsive alkalosis. Her history gives no obvious cause, so the test sorts the list. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 15-year-old soccer player has cough, chest tightness and wheezing that start within 3 minutes after hard running, peak about 10 to 15 minutes later, and clear within an hour. She has no symptoms at rest or at night. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":["Vocal fold dysfunction","Moderate persistent asthma","Catamenial asthma","Exercise-induced bronchospasm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Exercise-induced bronchospasm is common in children and adolescents, begins during or within 3 minutes after exercise, peaks in 10 to 15 minutes and resolves by 60 minutes, exactly this player's pattern, with no symptoms at rest or at night. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 94, 95"},{"q":"When is the local or state tuberculosis program notified?","choices":["When rifampin resistance is found","After treatment is completed","After culture confirmation arrives","For presumed or confirmed disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Immediate management of pulmonary tuberculosis starts with notifying the local or state tuberculosis program of presumed or confirmed disease, alongside airborne isolation when indicated and fit-tested N95 or higher respiratory protection for staff. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman reports severe breathlessness, stopping after about 100 meters on level ground, but her post-bronchodilator forced expiratory volume in 1 second is 82% predicted with a ratio of 0.68. Her heart evaluation is normal. Which step is most appropriate?","choices":["Reassurance with no follow-up","Triple inhaled therapy","Referral to pulmonology","Long-term oxygen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Her dyspnea grade of 3 is far worse than her near-normal airflow (grade 1, 82%) would explain. Symptoms out of proportion to pulmonary function tests, like diagnostic uncertainty, is a listed reason for pulmonology referral. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 24, 34, 54"},{"q":"A 57-year-old smoker has chronic cough and mild breathlessness. Post-bronchodilator spirometry shows a ratio of forced expiratory volume in 1 second to forced vital capacity of 0.76. How should this result be read?","choices":["It shows grade 2 disease","It confirms the disease","It does not confirm the disease","It proves asthma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis requires a post-bronchodilator ratio below 0.70, and his is 0.76. Chronic bronchitis without airflow obstruction is not chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, though smoking cessation remains essential for him. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 6, 26"},{"q":"Sodium 140 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145), chloride 104 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106) and bicarbonate 12 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26). What is the calculated anion gap?","choices":["12","24","36","48"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Anion gap = sodium - (chloride + bicarbonate) = 140 - (104 + 12) = 140 - 116 = 24. That is above the normal 9 to 15, so this is an elevated anion gap, as expected with such a low bicarbonate. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman with long-standing asthma has had repeated exacerbations this year despite confirmed adherence and good inhaler technique. Her clinician suspects a hypersensitivity reaction to a mold. Which result supports that suspicion?","choices":["Negative skin tests with a normal immunoglobulin E","Normal antibody levels to Aspergillus","A raised exhaled nitric oxide by itself","Raised immunoglobulin E and G to Aspergillus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis is a hypersensitivity reaction to Aspergillus fumigatus seen almost exclusively in asthma or cystic fibrosis. Exposure causes a vigorous immunoglobulin E and G response that leads to acute symptoms and exacerbations, as in this woman. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with bronchiectasis has had a chronic cough with sputum for months. Her sputum acid-fast bacilli smear is positive. What does this smear result establish?","choices":["Nontuberculous mycobacterial lung disease","Latent tuberculosis infection","Acid-fast organisms, species not yet known","Pulmonary tuberculosis disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An acid-fast smear identifies a group of organisms, the acid-fast bacilli with lipid-rich, mycolic acid cell walls. It does not distinguish Mycobacterium tuberculosis from nontuberculous mycobacteria, and bronchiectasis raises the risk of nontuberculous disease, so the species still has to be identified. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 4, 5"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man admitted with nonsevere community-acquired pneumonia is improving on ceftriaxone plus azithromycin. On day 3 his blood cultures grow Streptococcus pneumoniae that is susceptible to narrower agents. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Continue both for 14 days","Switch to cefepime","Narrow to the culture results","Add vancomycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antibiotics are reassessed daily and narrowed when possible, and therapy is narrowed to culture and susceptibility results. He is improving and his organism and its susceptibilities are known, so broad empiric coverage is no longer needed; duration follows clinical stability. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 38, 40, 45"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with community-acquired pneumonia is alert and oriented. Her blood urea nitrogen is 26 mg/dL, respiratory rate 24/min and blood pressure 128/76 mm Hg. What is her CURB-65 (confusion, urea, respiratory rate, blood pressure, age 65 or older) score?","choices":["1","2","3","4"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Confusion 0 (alert and oriented) + urea 1 (26 mg/dL is above 20) + respiratory rate 0 (24 is below 30) + blood pressure 0 (systolic not below 90, diastolic not 60 or lower) + age 1 (72) = 2. A total of 2 is moderate severity, with a risk of death of 9%, and the pathway sends her to the hospital. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 29, 31"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease was intubated with a carbon dioxide of 78 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), and the ventilator brought it to 38 within a few hours. He has had no vomiting, nasogastric suction, diuretics or antacids. The next morning: pH 7.55 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 38 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg), bicarbonate 32 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What best explains his alkalemia?","choices":["Worsening chronic respiratory acidosis","Post-hypercapnic metabolic alkalosis","Acute respiratory alkalosis","Renal compensation that overshot"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His kidneys had retained bicarbonate for his chronic hypercapnia; when the carbon dioxide was lowered quickly, that bicarbonate of 32 was left unopposed, a post-hypercapnic metabolic alkalosis. This is why rapid, aggressive carbon dioxide reduction is avoided in chronic respiratory acidosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with heart failure is started on oxygen. An hour later her arterial oxygen pressure has risen, but her arterial carbon dioxide has also risen and her pH has fallen. How should this response be read?","choices":["A good response: pH is moving","Not a good response: carbon dioxide is rising","Cannot be judged without a chest film","A good response: oxygen pressure has risen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A good response needs the arterial oxygen pressure to rise without a rise in arterial carbon dioxide, along with correction of the pH. Hers rose, but so did her carbon dioxide while the pH fell, so the oxygen is not working as it should and she needs close reassessment. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man starts treatment for macrolide-susceptible Mycobacterium avium complex lung disease and asks how long he will need it. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["12 months past culture conversion","6 months after symptoms resolve","Until his chest imaging is normal","12 months from the first dose"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Treatment continues for at least 12 months after culture conversion, so the clock starts when cultures turn negative, not with the first dose. That makes the whole course longer than 12 months. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is admitted with an exacerbation. She is alert and hemodynamically stable, but her blood gas shows rising carbon dioxide with respiratory acidosis despite bronchodilators, corticosteroids and antibiotics. Which support is most appropriate?","choices":["Discharge with albuterol","High-flow nasal cannula","Noninvasive ventilation","Long-term azithromycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Noninvasive ventilation is used for hypercapnic respiratory failure in an exacerbation and reduces intubation rates and mortality. Her alertness and stable circulation make her a candidate, and it is one of the 3 therapies with a survival benefit. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 58, 66"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has attacks of severe one-sided headache around his eye, each lasting under an hour, several days in a row. His saturation is 99%. Which therapy is a listed special indication for him?","choices":["A bag-valve mask","Oxygen therapy","Nebulized saline","No oxygen, since he is not hypoxic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Severe one-sided headaches around the eye, brief and recurring daily for days, fit cluster headache, which is a listed special indication for oxygen. His saturation of 99% does not matter, because the indication here is the headache, not hypoxia. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man with untreated human immunodeficiency virus infection cleaned out a barn full of bird droppings 3 weeks ago. He is admitted to the intensive care unit with high fever, hypoxemia, hypotension and diffuse infiltrates, and his urine Histoplasma antigen is positive. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Amphotericin-based induction","Observation without an antifungal","Oral itraconazole as an outpatient","Fluconazole for 6 to 12 months"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Histoplasmosis treatment follows severity and host risk. An intensive care admission with hypoxemia, hypotension and diffuse infiltrates in an immunocompromised host is severe disease, and severe pulmonary or disseminated histoplasmosis requires specialist-led amphotericin-based induction. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 75, 77, 78, 123"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with a lung abscess has had two episodes of Clostridioides difficile colitis in the past year. Which part of a lung abscess plan should be avoided for her?","choices":["Clindamycin","Ampicillin-sulbactam","Amoxicillin-clavulanate","Several weeks of treatment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Clindamycin is reserved for selected alternatives because of Clostridioides difficile risk, and she has had two episodes of that colitis. Her abscess is treated with a beta-lactam/beta-lactamase inhibitor instead, intravenous ampicillin-sulbactam followed by oral amoxicillin-clavulanate. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is admitted with a flare. His oxygen saturation is 86% on room air, and the team wants to give him an exact, adjustable fraction of inspired oxygen rather than a fraction that changes with his breathing. Which delivery device is the best choice?","choices":["Venturi mask","Simple mask","Non-rebreather mask","Nasal cannula"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. He has chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and the team wants a precise, adjustable fraction of inspired oxygen that does not change with his breathing. The Venturi mask is an air-entrainment device: different ports set the fraction (24-50%), and it stays fixed whatever his demand. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman began hyperventilating at a high-altitude resort this afternoon. Before her kidneys respond, what is the initial compensation for her respiratory alkalosis?","choices":["Cells release hydrogen ions for potassium","She hypoventilates to retain carbon dioxide","The kidneys excrete bicarbonate","The kidneys retain bicarbonate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The initial response to respiratory alkalosis is cellular secretion of hydrogen ions in exchange for potassium. Later the kidneys excrete bicarbonate and retain chloride, lowering bicarbonate, potassium and raising chloride. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 43-year-old woman with suspected pulmonary tuberculosis has three sputum specimens collected. Which testing should be requested on the specimens?","choices":["Acid-fast smear, then stop if negative","Acid-fast smear, culture and a molecular test","Gram stain and routine bacterial culture","Molecular test in place of culture"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The specimens go for acid-fast smear and mycobacterial culture, a rapid molecular test is performed on at least one respiratory specimen, and molecular and culture-based drug-susceptibility testing is obtained. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has a positive interferon-gamma release assay on targeted testing. He has no symptoms. Which next step is most appropriate?","choices":["Clinical evaluation and chest imaging","Start latent-infection treatment today","Repeat the assay in 8 to 10 weeks","Airborne isolation and health reporting"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A positive interferon-gamma release assay indicates infection, but neither it nor the skin test can distinguish latent infection from disease. A positive result requires clinical evaluation and chest imaging, so disease is excluded before any latent-infection treatment. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 19, 24"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman started an inhaled corticosteroid for moderate persistent asthma 1 week ago and wants to stop it because she does not feel much different yet. What should she be told?","choices":["It is meant to be used during attacks","Her lack of change means she needs oral steroids","It should work within minutes","Its full effect can take months"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Inhaled corticosteroids are the preferred first-line treatment for persistent asthma, and most patients respond to twice-daily dosing, but the maximum response may take months. One week is too early to judge, and stopping would leave her inflammation untreated. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman with community-acquired pneumonia has a Pneumonia Severity Index total of 82 points. After intravenous fluids she remains hypotensive and needs a vasopressor infusion to maintain her blood pressure. She is breathing on her own. Where should she be treated?","choices":["A general ward, by her score","An observation unit for a repeat score","Intensive care unit-level care","Outpatient care with close follow-up"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vasopressor-dependent shock is one of the severity signals of severe community-acquired pneumonia, with mechanical ventilation or at least three minor criteria, and severe pneumonia needs urgent intensive care-level assessment and circulatory support. The Pneumonia Severity Index guides the outpatient-or-ward choice only in nonsevere pneumonia. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 30, 45"},{"q":"What does the modified Medical Research Council dyspnea scale grade?","choices":["Breathlessness","Airflow obstruction","Four-year survival","Exacerbation risk"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The modified Medical Research Council scale grades breathlessness from 0 (breathless only with strenuous exercise) to 4 (too breathless to leave the house, or breathless dressing). A grade of 2 or more counts as more symptoms. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 24, 52"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with septic shock and lactic acidosis is tiring. His bicarbonate is 12 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L) and his carbon dioxide is 38 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg). By the Winter formula, what does his carbon dioxide show?","choices":["Normal, since 38 is in range","An added respiratory acidosis","An added respiratory alkalosis","Appropriate compensation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Winter formula: 1.5 x 12 + 8 = 26, plus or minus 2, so 24 to 28. His carbon dioxide of 38 is well above it: metabolic acidosis together with respiratory acidosis, which can signal impending respiratory failure in a tiring patient. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man has a post-bronchodilator ratio of 0.55 and a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 34% predicted. Which airflow grade fits?","choices":["Grade 1 (mild)","Grade 2 (moderate)","Grade 3 (severe)","Grade 4 (very severe)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With the ratio below 0.70, the grade comes from the post-bronchodilator 1-second volume: 34% sits in the 30% to 49% band, grade 3, severe. Grade 4 would need less than 30%. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman with no medical conditions has community-acquired pneumonia and is stable for outpatient treatment. She has no risk for resistant organisms, and local pneumococcal resistance to macrolides is 30%. Which antibiotic is most appropriate?","choices":["Levofloxacin","Cefpodoxime plus azithromycin","Azithromycin as a single drug","Amoxicillin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With no comorbidities and no resistant-organism risk, the outpatient choices are amoxicillin or doxycycline. Macrolide monotherapy is used only if local pneumococcal resistance is below 25%, and hers is 30%, so amoxicillin fits. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with no chronic conditions received the 15-valent pneumococcal conjugate vaccine 14 months ago at a pharmacy and has had no pneumococcal vaccine since. Which plan completes her pneumococcal vaccination?","choices":["Nothing; her series is complete","A second 15-valent conjugate dose","A 23-valent dose when she turns 65","A 23-valent polysaccharide dose now"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Every adult aged 50 or older is eligible, and a series started with the 15-valent conjugate vaccine usually needs the 23-valent polysaccharide vaccine afterward, usually 1 year or more later. Fourteen months have passed, so she gets it now. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 8, 9, 124"},{"q":"A 15-year-old soccer player develops cough and wheezing within minutes of hard running, peaking about 10 minutes later and resolving within an hour. She is otherwise symptom-free. Which treatment should she use before exercise?","choices":["Long-acting beta-2 agonist alone","Tiotropium","Inhaled short-acting beta-2 agonist","A short oral corticosteroid course"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Her symptoms begin within minutes of exercise, peak at 10 to 15 minutes and resolve by an hour, the exercise-induced bronchospasm pattern. An inhaled short-acting beta-2 agonist used before exercise decreases the bronchoconstriction. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 94, 96"},{"q":"Which antibody response drives the asthma symptoms of allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis?","choices":["An immunoglobulin A response at the mucosa","An immunoglobulin M response","Absent antibodies with normal immunity","A vigorous immunoglobulin E and G response"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Exposure to Aspergillus causes a vigorous immunoglobulin E and immunoglobulin G antibody response, leading to acute asthma symptoms or an exacerbation in a patient with asthma or cystic fibrosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman with diabetic ketoacidosis has a bicarbonate of 10 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L) and a carbon dioxide of 22 mm Hg (normal 36 to 44 mm Hg). By the Winter formula, how is her respiratory compensation?","choices":["Too little: added respiratory acidosis","It cannot be judged without the gap","Appropriate","Too much: added respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Winter formula: expected carbon dioxide = 1.5 x 10 + 8 = 23, plus or minus 2, so 21 to 25. Her measured 22 falls inside that range, so this is a simple, appropriately compensated metabolic acidosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 14-year-old girl with moderate persistent asthma comes in for her yearly influenza vaccine, and her mother asks whether she can have the nasal spray form instead of a shot. Which vaccine form calls for caution in her?","choices":["Any influenza vaccine this year","The intranasal influenza vaccine","The pneumonia vaccine","Every vaccine during asthma treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Annual influenza vaccination is recommended because vaccine-preventable illness can trigger asthma exacerbations, but intranasal influenza vaccines call for caution, since they can be associated with exacerbation in younger patients such as this 14-year-old. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"What is the most common reason for poor symptom control in refractory asthma?","choices":["Steroid-resistant asthma","Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis","Exercise-induced bronchospasm","Non-compliance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Refractory asthma takes two forms, persistent symptoms with poor lung function or normal function with severe exacerbations; non-compliance is the most common reason for poor symptom control, and steroid-resistant asthma is very uncommon. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man admitted with a heart failure exacerbation still needs 2 L/min of oxygen on hospital day 1. His team plans to discharge him in a few days. When should his home oxygen qualification be performed?","choices":["Once he no longer needs oxygen","At the time of need, before discharge","At his first outpatient visit","After a year on home oxygen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The qualification is documented at the time of need, and he still needs 2 L/min with discharge planned in a few days. So the qualification is performed while he is in the hospital, before he leaves, so that home oxygen can be arranged. — 35. Oxygen Delivery 2026.pptx, Slide 5"}]};
